---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="How it works — Runtime-Isolated Gates"
  description="RIG is Runtime-Isolated Gates: staged work, a mechanical gate at every handoff, and an independent verdict after every step."
>
  <section class="nf-section">
    <div class="nf-content">
      <h1>What RIG is</h1>
      <p>
        Work runs in isolated stages. Every handoff between them passes a mechanical
        gate, and every stage's output is judged by something that did not produce it.
        The architecture that does this is called RIG — Runtime-Isolated Gates. The
        adjective is "isolated": the gates sit between runtimes that share nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each step starts fresh with only the context it needs. Nothing carries over
        implicitly, so nothing drifts across a long session and no earlier mistake rides
        along unexamined into a later one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="nf-section">
    <div class="nf-content">
      <h2>The loop</h2>
      <p>One vocabulary, defined here and used exactly.</p>
      <p>
        A <strong>stage</strong> is one unit of work with declared inputs and declared outputs. A
        <strong>fitting</strong> is one process inside a stage: a single invocation with its own
        instruction, its own file permissions, and its own budget. A <strong>feed</strong> is the
        file a stage hands to the next stage — the only thing that crosses a stage
        boundary. A <strong>gate</strong> is a mechanical check, run before a stage starts, that the
        keys the stage requires are present in the feed it was handed. A <strong>verdict</strong> is
        the file a verifier writes: a status of pass, halt, or fail, and one sentence of
        reason.
      </p>
      <p>Every stage runs the same loop:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Gate.</strong> The feed from the previous stage is checked against the keys this
          stage requires. A missing key stops the run before any work begins.</li>
        <li><strong>Work.</strong> The fitting reads what its contract names, writes what its contract
          names, and nothing else.</li>
        <li><strong>Verdict.</strong> A separate invocation reads the output and writes a status and a
          reason. It judges; it does not repair.</li>
        <li><strong>Branch.</strong> Pass continues to the next stage. Halt and fail stop the run and
          leave every artifact on disk.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        A <strong>halt</strong> and a <strong>fail</strong> are different events. A halt means the plumbing is
        suspect: something upstream did not arrive in the shape the next stage was
        promised. A fail means the plumbing worked and the work itself was judged bad.
        They get fixed in different places, so they are named differently.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="nf-section">
    <div class="nf-content nf-content--wide">
      <h2>Jidoka for AI</h2>
      <p>
        The frame is jidoka: the line stops at the defect, the defect never travels
        downstream, and the stop is information rather than failure. The mapping is
        direct.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>On the factory line</th><th>In the pipeline</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>The andon cord — any worker can stop the line</td><td>The verdict file. Any verifier stops the run, and the controller has no override.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Stop-the-line — defects never travel downstream</td><td>Halt and fail semantics. A defect at one stage never becomes the input to the next.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Go and see the actual thing</td><td>The preserved artifact. Diagnosis happens from the failing file on disk, never from a description of it.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fix the source, not the piece</td><td>A fail sends you to the instruction or the rubric, not to hand-editing the output. Repeated hand-edits of the same kind mean the process is underspecified.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>No rework-and-hope</td><td>No automatic retry. A retry that happens to pass hides a defect that will come back.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="nf-section">
    <div class="nf-content">
      <h2>Where every handoff is inspected</h2>
      <p>
        A stage does not advance because nobody objected. It advances because a checker
        affirmatively passed it, and the checker is structurally separate from the work:
        workers never grade their own output, verifiers cannot edit what they judge, and
        the controller that sequences the run cannot think about the content at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        That separation is the whole mechanism. The judgment runs in its own runtime,
        with its own context, so it does not inherit the assumptions that produced the
        work it is reading.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="nf-section">
    <div class="nf-content">
      <h2>Where the analogy breaks</h2>
      <p>
        Stated plainly, because a manufacturing-literate reader will test the analogy
        here first.
      </p>
      <p>
        The factory frame is built around continuous flow and takt time. This is a batch
        machine by design: local-first, sequential, one run at a time.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kaizen's worker-driven improvement loop has no analog here yet. Improvement is a
        human reading the logs and revising the fittings.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="nf-section">
    <div class="nf-content">
      <h2>What the run records</h2>
      <p>
        Every invocation writes down what it was asked to do, what it produced, what it
        cost, and what was decided about it. Failed runs are preserved rather than
        cleaned up: the failing feed, the gate's error line, and the verdict stay on
        disk as the diagnosis.
      </p>
      <p>
        This site was built and verified on RIG, no.fail's pipeline architecture: every
        stage gated before it ran, every stage's output verified after it ran. The loop
        described above is that pipeline. A white paper drawing on the run's records is
        forthcoming.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="nf-section">
    <div class="nf-content">
      <h2>Next</h2>
      <p>Tell us what your team does by hand that it shouldn't.</p>
      <a class="nf-btn" href="/contact/">Start there</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
